 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "An exponential equation has the unknown in the index: 2ˣ = 32.\n• Write both sides as powers of the same base: 2ˣ = 2⁵.\n• If the bases are equal, the indices are equal: x = 5.\n• 3^(x + 1) = 81 = 3⁴ → x + 1 = 4 → x = 3.\n• Different bases: 4ˣ = 8 → (2²)ˣ = 2³ → 2x = 3 → x = 3/2.\n• Fractions: 5ˣ = 1/25 = 5⁻² → x = −2.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "learn": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "sections": [
   {
    "heading": "The unknown in the index",
    "text": "In {2}ˣ = {32}, x tells how many {2}s are multiplied. Count: {2}, {4}, {8}, {16}, {32}: five of them, so x = {5}.\nIn general: write both sides as powers of the same base. If {2}ˣ = {pw(2, 5)}, the bases are the same, so the indices must be equal: x = {5}."
   },
   {
    "heading": "When the index is an expression",
    "text": "{3}^(x + {1}) = {81}. Write {81} = {pw(3, 4)}, so x + {1} = {4} and x = {3}.\nWith a different base, look for a common base: {4}ˣ = {8}. Both are powers of {2}: {4} = {pw(2, 2)} and {8} = {pw(2, 3)}.\nSo {2}^({2}x) = {pw(2, 3)}, {mono(2, 'x')} = {3} and x = {frac(3, 2)}."
   },
   {
    "heading": "Fractions and negative answers",
    "text": "If the right-hand side is a fraction with a power on the bottom, use a negative index:\n{5}ˣ = {frac(1, 125)} = {5}⁻{sup(3)}, so x = {-3}.\nAnd {7}ˣ = {1} gives x = {0}, because any base to the power zero is {1}."
   }
  ],
  "key_words": [
   {
    "word": "exponential equation",
    "meaning": "an equation with the unknown in the index"
   },
   {
    "word": "common base",
    "meaning": "a number of which both sides are powers"
   }
  ],
  "examples": [
   "{10}ˣ = {1000} → x = {3}",
   "{9}ˣ = {27} → x = {frac(3, 2)}",
   "{2}ˣ = {frac(1, 8)} → x = {-3}"
  ],
  "mistake": {
   "wrong": "{2}ˣ = {16} → x = {8}",
   "right": "{16} = {pw(2, 4)}, so x = {4}",
   "why": "x is the number of {2}s multiplied together, not {16} ÷ {2}."
  }
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "p": 2,
   "m": 2,
   "n": 3
  },
  "problem": "Solve {p ^ m}ˣ = {p ^ n}.",
  "steps": [
   "Write both as powers of {p}: {p ^ m} = {p}{sup(m)} and {p ^ n} = {p}{sup(n)}.",
   "({p}{sup(m)})ˣ = {p}{sup(n)}, so {p}^({m}x) = {p}{sup(n)}.",
   "Equal bases, equal indices: {mono(m, 'x')} = {n}, so x = {frac(n, m)}."
  ],
  "answer": "x = {frac(n, m)}",
  "checks": [
   "n / m == 1.5"
  ]
 },
